text:
An intern is a temporary employee engaged for a defined program, typically 10 to 16 weeks. Interns are paid hourly and are W-2, T4, or PAYE employees. Interns:

- do not accrue PTO, but receive sick leave (5 days per program) and paid company holidays that fall within their program dates;
- are not eligible for medical, dental, vision, the 401(k) match, stipends, tuition reimbursement, or the learning budget;
- are not eligible for remote work arrangements outside their assigned office, except with written approval from the University Programs lead;
- may use the Employee Assistance Program.

A contractor is an independent contractor or a worker supplied by an agency. Contractors are not employees of Lumora. Their working arrangements, time off, equipment, and expenses are governed entirely by their contract or statement of work. Contractors:

- are not eligible for PTO, company benefits, stipends, the learning budget, or the work-from-abroad program;
- may be reimbursed for expenses only where the statement of work explicitly allows it;
- must follow the Information Security Policy (POL-006) and the Workplace Conduct Policy (POL-012) while engaged;
- may use the Employee Assistance Program while actively engaged.

Questions about contractor arrangements must be directed to the Vendor Management team, not People Operations.

All new employees (full-time, part-time, and interns) complete a 90-day introductory period that begins on their start date. During the introductory period:

- employees accrue PTO from their first day but may not use more than 3 days of PTO before day 90 without manager and HR approval;
- employees are not eligible to request remote work from a location other than their assigned office or home address (see POL-004, Section 4);
- employees are not eligible for the work-from-abroad program (see POL-005);
- managers complete 30-, 60-, and 90-day check-ins as described in the Onboarding Guide (POL-010).

To request a hybrid or remote arrangement, or to request temporary work from another location, an employee must:

1. be a full-time or part-time employee (interns and contractors are not eligible; see POL-001);
2. have completed the 90-day introductory period;
3. not be on a formal performance improvement plan; and
4. hold a remote-eligible role.

Employees working remotely, on a hybrid basis, or from a temporary location must:

- be reachable during their team's core collaboration hours (10:00 a.m. to 3:00 p.m. in the team's primary time zone);
- maintain a safe, quiet workspace suitable for confidential conversations;
- use only Lumora-issued devices for Company work and connect through the Company VPN when on any network other than a Lumora office network (see POL-006, Section 5);
- never work from public locations on Restricted data (see POL-006, Section 3);
- keep their PeopleHub address and emergency contact up to date;
- report any work-related injury at home to People Operations within 24 hours.

To use the WFA program, an employee must:

1. be a full-time or part-time employee (interns and contractors are not eligible; see POL-001);
2. have completed the 90-day introductory period;
3. hold a remote-eligible role (POL-004, Section 3.1);
4. be a citizen of, or already hold the legal right to work in, the destination country or be traveling to a country where Global Mobility has confirmed that short remote work is permitted for visitors of the employee's nationality; and
5. not be traveling to a restricted country (Section 6).

While working abroad, employees must:

- use only their Lumora-issued device or a travel loaner issued by IT;
- connect through the Company VPN at all times;
- not access Restricted data (POL-006, Section 3) from outside their home country unless Information Security has approved it in writing;
- never leave devices unattended in hotel rooms, vehicles, or public places;
- report a lost or stolen device to the IT Service Desk within 1 hour of discovery (POL-006, Section 9).

Access to some customer systems is blocked from outside the employee's home country by contractual requirements. Employees should confirm with their manager that they can do their full job from the destination before requesting WFA.

Every worker is responsible for:

- protecting their credentials and never sharing passwords, MFA codes, or access tokens;
- handling data according to its classification (Section 3);
- using only approved devices and software (Sections 4 and 7);
- reporting security incidents promptly (Section 9);
- completing annual security awareness training within 30 days of assignment. New hires complete training within their first 7 days (see Onboarding Guide, POL-010).

Report any of the following to the IT Service Desk or the #security-incidents channel within 1 hour of discovery:

- a lost or stolen device or security key;
- a suspected phishing compromise, or credentials entered on a suspicious site;
- Confidential or Restricted data sent to the wrong person or posted publicly;
- malware warnings or unusual device behavior.

Reporting quickly matters more than knowing whether something is really an incident. Employees who report in good faith will not be disciplined for the report itself.

The following are never reimbursed:

- personal computers, laptops, tablets, and phones (Lumora issues these through IT; see POL-011);
- personal entertainment, including in-flight entertainment and hotel movies;
- alcohol, except as part of approved client entertainment (Section 5.5);
- traffic and parking fines, and penalties of any kind;
- seat, class, or room upgrades that are not covered in Section 5;
- personal grooming, clothing, and luggage;
- gifts to colleagues, except through the recognition program;
- travel, lodging, or living costs during personal Work From Abroad periods (POL-005, Section 10);
- expenses of family members or guests.

During your first week you should:

- complete Security Awareness training (required within 7 days of your start date);
- complete Workplace Conduct and Anti-Harassment training (required within 14 days; managers have additional manager training within 30 days);
- enroll in benefits in PeopleHub (you have 30 days from your start date; see Benefits Overview, POL-008, Section 3);
- review your 401(k) auto-enrollment and change your contribution rate if you wish;
- read the PTO Policy (POL-002) and add company holidays for your location (POL-003) to your calendar;
- agree on your 30-60-90 day goals with your manager;
- request any additional equipment, such as an external monitor for hybrid and remote employees, through the IT Service Desk (POL-011).

Managers must:

- prepare an onboarding plan and share it with the new hire on day one;
- assign an onboarding buddy;
- hold 30-, 60-, and 90-day check-ins and record them in PeopleHub;
- ensure required training is completed on time;
- raise concerns about fit or performance with People Operations before day 75 so there is time to address them.

Every employee and intern receives:

- one laptop: a 14-inch MacBook Pro or a ThinkPad X1 Carbon, depending on role. Engineers and designers may request a 16-inch model with manager approval;
- a laptop charger and a USB-C hub;
- a hardware security key (two for engineers with production access);
- a headset with microphone.

Hybrid and remote employees may also request, through the IT Service Desk:

- one 27-inch external monitor, shipped to their registered home address;
- a full-size keyboard and mouse.

Office-based employees have monitors, docks, keyboards, and mice at their desks or in hot-desk areas.

Contractors receive equipment only when their statement of work requires it; otherwise they must use a Lumora-managed virtual desktop.

Everyone at Lumora is expected to:
- treat colleagues, customers, and partners with respect and courtesy;
- communicate professionally in all channels, including chat and video calls;
- avoid conflicts of interest and disclose any potential conflict to their manager and Legal;
- protect Company and customer information (see POL-006);
- comply with all laws and Company policies;
- speak up when they see behavior that violates this policy.

Many questions can be answered from the policy library and PeopleHub. Employees should check:

- PeopleHub for their own PTO balance, benefits elections, pay statements, and work arrangement;
- the policy library for rules and eligibility;
- the HR Assistant, which answers policy questions with citations to the policy library and can look up the employee's own records.

When self-service does not resolve the question, employees should open an HR ticket.

A request must be escalated to a human People Operations partner, rather than answered from policy alone, when:

1. it involves possible harassment, discrimination, bullying, retaliation, violence, or a safety risk;
2. the employee describes a medical condition, disability, or accommodation need;
3. the answer depends on a judgment about a specific person's conduct or performance;
4. the policy is silent or ambiguous for the employee's situation, or two policies appear to conflict;
5. the employee disputes a decision already made by their manager or People Operations;
6. the request involves pay corrections, legal matters, immigration, or tax advice;
7. the employee asks for an exception to a policy.

If anyone is in immediate danger, the employee should contact local emergency services first.

The Lumora HR Assistant and any other automated tool used by People Operations must:

- answer policy questions only from the official policy library and cite the document ID and section for each policy fact;
- clearly separate policy facts from recommendations or next steps;
- say when the policy library does not cover a question, rather than guessing, and suggest opening a ticket;
- show employees only their own records;
- never submit, approve, or change a PeopleHub request, and never create a ticket or send a message on the employee's behalf without the employee's explicit confirmation;
- never investigate, make findings about, or give opinions on the merits of an Employee Relations matter; instead, explain the reporting channels and offer to open a confidential Employee Relations case;
- never give personal tax, legal, immigration, or medical advice.

To help People Operations resolve a ticket quickly, include:

- your employee ID;
- the category;
- a short summary of the request and the relevant dates;
- the policy sections you have already reviewed;
- any documents (upload them in the ticket, not by email).

Do not include medical details in a ticket unless it is a Leave or Accommodations ticket, which are restricted to the relevant team.
